<GuideLayout
  title="How Much Yarn Do I Need?"
  description="Learn how to estimate yarn for blankets, sweaters, hats, and scarves using area, Craft Yarn Council weights, and swatch weighing."
  path="/guides/how-much-yarn-do-i-need/"
  answerFirst="Yarn need is mostly finished area × yarn thickness. A 50×60 in throw in worsted (CYC 4) often needs about 2,000–2,600 yards; an adult beanie may need only 110–200 yards. Use category averages to shop, then confirm with a swatch weight or a pattern’s tested yardage."
>
  <h2>Start with the project shape</h2>
  <p>
    Rectangles (blankets, scarves) are easiest: convert width and length to area, then apply a meters-per-square-meter factor for your yarn weight.
    Use our <a href="/tools/yarn-blanket-calculator/">blanket</a>, <a href="/tools/yarn-scarf-calculator/">scarf</a>,
    <a href="/tools/yarn-hat-calculator/">hat</a>, and <a href="/tools/yarn-sweater-calculator/">sweater</a> calculators for instant estimates.
  </p>
  <h2>Use Craft Yarn Council categories</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://www.craftyarncouncil.com/standards/yarn-weight-system">CYC Standard Yarn Weight System</a> groups yarn from 0 (lace) to 7 (jumbo)
    with typical gauges and needle ranges. Thicker yarn needs fewer meters per square meter of fabric but more grams per meter.
    Always prefer the meters printed on the ball band when comparing brands.
  </p>
  <h2>Knit vs crochet</h2>
  <p>
    Dense single crochet usually consumes more yarn than stockinette for the same finished size—often roughly 15–25% more.
    If you switch crafts mid-plan, recalculate rather than reusing a knit estimate.
  </p>
  <h2>The swatch weighing method</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Knit or crochet a blocked swatch; record its area.</li>
    <li>Weigh the swatch on a kitchen scale (grams).</li>
    <li>Scale: project grams ≈ swatch grams × (project area ÷ swatch area), then add 10%.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>When to trust a pattern instead</h2>
  <p>
    Designer yardage from a sample garment beats geometric estimates for complex shaping, cables, and colorwork.
    Use calculators for stash shopping, substitutions (<a href="/tools/yarn-substitution-calculator/">substitution tool</a>), and custom sizes.
  </p>
